  "editorial_note": "GitHub listed twelve projects this Monday morning, and eight of them were featured here in the past week, so they stay out. Today's four picks are all first appearances: ggml, the ==tensor library under llama.cpp, which just reached 1.0.0==; NVIDIA's SkillEvaluator for testing whether agent skills actually help; Claude-BugHunter, a skill bundle for authorized bug hunting; and Google's Stitch skills for design work with coding agents. Curation order reflects practical interest, not GitHub rank. Trending is not a release date or an endorsement.",
  "coverage_notes": [
    "GitHub's daily listing held 12 rows at capture. The capture parsed exactly twelve listing rows and is GitHub's own list, not a truncation by us.",
    "All 12 listing entries were inspected. Exact public GitHub API metadata and README files of the four new candidates were read between 07:01:13 and 07:01:19 UTC, and recent releases between 07:01:41 and 07:01:48 UTC. Every checked repository was active and unarchived.",
    "Eight entries featured in the past seven days were left out: GoogleChrome/modern-web-guidance-src (12 November), backnotprop/plannotator, ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp, alibaba/open-code-review and tradermonty/claude-trading-skills (14 November), and dream-num/univer, ag-ui-protocol/ag-ui and Tencent/LoopForge (15 November). Their releases since those picks are patch builds, dated routine releases or a release candidate, or none at all, which we did not judge a material change.",
    "Claude-BugHunter is dual-licensed by its README and NOTICE: MIT for code and CC BY 4.0 for skill content and documentation. The license field on its card shows GitHub's detection (MIT) only.",
    "The preceding seven calendar dates, 9-15 November, were checked for local history; all seven editions exist.",
    "The official listing was captured at 08:00:56 Europe/Paris. Literal stars-today labels are GitHub's displayed period figures; API total stars and README evidence were checked separately at the times shown per card. They are not an independently audited rolling 24-hour growth measurement.",
    "Feature descriptions, benchmark, adoption and performance figures come from each project's own README and release notes and are the maintainers' claims. No candidate code, installer, CLI, plugin, skill or model was installed, downloaded or run, and none of this is a security audit or endorsement."
  ],
  "projects": [
    {
      "repository": "ggml-org/ggml",
      "url": "https://github.com/ggml-org/ggml",
      "trending_rank": 8,
      "description": "The tensor library behind llama.cpp: plain C/C++ with no dependencies, it runs machine-learning models on CPUs, GPUs, NPUs and in the browser, with 2- to 8-bit quantization and the GGUF file format.",
      "why_interesting": "Version 1.0.0, published on 13 November, marks ==the first major version of the engine behind local AI==. It prunes the deprecated public API, adds FP8 support and configurable YaRN truncation, and lists broad backend work across CUDA, Metal, Vulkan, SYCL, OpenCL and WebGPU. Speed gains in the release notes are maintainer claims.",
      "caveat": "1.0.0 is ==a breaking release for anyone on the old API==: deprecated functions and the legacy DIAG_MASK ops are gone, and the RPC protocol bump rejects older peers. Core changes are made in the llama.cpp repository and synced here, so issues and pull requests belong there.",
      "category": "Machine learning infrastructure",
      "language": "C++",
      "license": "MIT",
      "stars_total": 16001,
      "stars_period": 43,
      "stars_period_label": "43 stars today",
